[THOUGHT] The fundoscopic finding — pale retina with a cherry-red spot at the macula — is pathognomonic for central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO). This confirms the diagnosis with high specificity. However, before issuing [FINISH], I must still complete the full eight-domain evidence framework per protocol. We have covered domains 1 (chief complaint: sudden painless monocular vision loss), 2 (course: persistent, no fluctuation), and 3 (associated symptoms: none neurologic or ophthalmic). Now I must systematically gather the remaining five DISCRIMINATING domains:  
4) Past medical & surgical history — especially hypertension, diabetes, atrial fibrillation, carotid stenosis, prior TIA/stroke, or vasculitis (e.g., giant cell arteritis, though typically painful and in older adults);  
5) Medications & allergies — anticoagulants, antiplatelets, recent new meds;  
6) Social/exposure history — smoking, recent travel, illicit drug use (e.g., cocaine), occupational exposures;  
7) Targeted physical exam — we need blood pressure (bilateral), heart auscultation for murmurs/rhythm, carotid auscultation for bruits, and visual acuity/afferent pupillary defect (APD) testing — which is critical: CRAO almost always causes a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) in the affected eye;  
8) Most informative labs/imaging — ECG (for AFib), urgent ESR/CRP if GCA suspected (though unlikely given age/painlessness), and carotid Doppler or cardiac echo if embolic source suspected.  

Since domain 7 (targeted physical exam) includes APD — a rapid, bedside, highly discriminative test for optic nerve/retinal ischemia — and it’s more immediately actionable than history questions, I’ll prioritize it now. APD testing takes seconds and directly corroborates retinal or optic nerve dysfunction.

[TEST] Perform swinging flashlight test to assess for a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) in the right eye.
